1
00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:03.603
In the last lesson, you

2
00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.606
used an LLM
to invoke a single function.

3
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:10.176
In this lesson, we're going to cover
all of the variations of this.

4
00:00:10.477 --> 00:00:13.113
All right. Let's go.

5
00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:15.949
There are a lot of different permutations
and combinations

6
00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:19.419
of functions and calls
that an LLM is capable of issuing.

7
00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:23.456
These include single calls,
parallel calls,

8
00:00:23.723 --> 00:00:27.127
no calls, multiple functions,
and nested functions.

9
00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:29.662
Let's start with parallel calls.

10
00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.532
Before we get started,
let's do some housekeeping.

11
00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:36.169
In the last lesson, you created a function
and then created

12
00:00:36.169 --> 00:00:39.239
a prompt with the description
of the function embedded within

13
00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:42.475
the prompt. You can actually be
a bit more efficient about it.

14
00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:47.313
By using the definition itself
to create the prompt automatically.

15
00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:51.785
To show this, you define
a generic function called "afunction".

16
00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.321
You can actually use the name and dot

17
00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:58.892
attributes like this
to pull out the relevant information,

18
00:00:59.626 --> 00:01:04.364
and you can use the Python inspect module
to get the signature

19
00:01:04.697 --> 00:01:07.700
or the arguments of the function
like this.

20
00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:11.971
You can put all of that together
in a utility that you can use

21
00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:15.141
to create prompts
for a given list of functions,

22
00:01:15.642 --> 00:01:18.812
such as this utility called "build
Raven prompt".

23
00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:22.415
Here, you are accepting a function
list and a user query

24
00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:25.552
for every function in your function list.

25
00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:29.322
You're using the trait we discussed
earlier to pull out the signature

26
00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:33.226
or the arguments of the function,
as well as its dot string,

27
00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:36.429
and you're using that
to create the function annotation

28
00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:39.232
for each of the functions
in the function list.

29
00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:41.768
And then adding the user query at the end.

30
00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:46.272
Let's try it on our example.

31
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:48.108
Great.

32
00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:49.709
That prompt looks fine.

33
00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:50.210
Okay.

34
00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:54.614
Now, in this example, you will use
the function from the first lesson.

35
00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:59.486
We've defined this in the utils
API file to keep our notebooks small.

36
00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:04.524
If you wish to view this file, please
click on view. Under view,

37
00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:09.129
please click on File Browser
to open the list of files available.

38
00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.633
Please click on utils.py
to open the utilities file

39
00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:16.636
that contains all the functions
we'll be describing.

40
00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:22.108
This function now has more parameters
to make it more complex.

41
00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:26.813
Previously we had the face
color, eye color, and the nose color.

42
00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:29.849
However,
we've now extended it to include more

43
00:02:29.849 --> 00:02:33.486
parameterizations of the clown's face,
such as the eyes size,

44
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.158
mouth size, mouth color, eye offset,
and the mouth theta.

45
00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:42.362
These controlled various attributes,
such as the width and the height

46
00:02:42.362 --> 00:02:46.533
of the clowns mouth,
as well as the starting and ending angles.

47
00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.771
They also control the offsets of the eyes
as well as the size of the eyes.

48
00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.907
Now back to parallel calls.

49
00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.543
Parallel calls are when the

50
00:02:56.543 --> 00:03:01.347
LLM has to issue in the same
turn, multiple function call strings

51
00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:04.584
to either the same function
or to a set of functions.

52
00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:07.320
Let's take a look at one example.

53
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.323
You will use a user query that,
while similar,

54
00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:13.960
is more complex than the user query
we used in the first lesson.

55
00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:17.397
In this user
query, you're asking for two clowns,

56
00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:20.800
one with a red face
and one with a blue face,

57
00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:24.871
one with a blue nose
and the other with a green nose.

58
00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:28.308
And the first clown
should have a sad smile.

59
00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:31.311
And the second clown
should have a happy smile.

60
00:03:31.644 --> 00:03:33.780
You will soon see why we specify

61
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.684
the numerical representations
instead of just saying happy or sad.

62
00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:40.620
But for now, let's just keep it as it is.

63
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:44.824
You saw that the new clown face
function is far more complex than earlier.

64
00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:48.795
You can now build the Raven prompt
by passing

65
00:03:48.795 --> 00:03:51.965
in the clown face
function and your user query.

66
00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.941
This looks as we would expect.

67
00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:04.944
Here is the function delineator.

68
00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:07.680
Here is the function itself.

69
00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.184
And here is the description
of the arguments of the function.

70
00:04:12.385 --> 00:04:15.388
And finally a description of what
the function does.

71
00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.958
Let's call Raven.

72
00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:20.560
Great.

73
00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.563
You can see that
there are two function calls here.

74
00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.865
Here's one. And here's the other.

75
00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:28.568
The first calls for a clown
with a red face.

76
00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:31.571
The second one for a clown
with the blue face.

77
00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.707
The first one calls for a clown
with a blue nose.

78
00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:37.577
The second one with a green nose.

79
00:04:37.577 --> 00:04:39.479
The first one for a sad smile.

80
00:04:39.479 --> 00:04:42.015
And the second one for a happy smile.

81
00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:46.986
And we see that the clown matches the
descriptions we asked in the user query.

82
00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:48.721
Try it yourself.

83
00:04:48.721 --> 00:04:52.692
Try requesting three or more faces
with different features.

84
00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.428
That was parallel functions.

85
00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:57.997
Now let's talk about multiple functions.

86
00:04:57.997 --> 00:05:01.167
You can provide the LLM
with multiple functions

87
00:05:01.167 --> 00:05:04.837
such as f1, f2, f3, f4 all the way to fN,

88
00:05:05.238 --> 00:05:07.974
and the LLM should be able to pick

89
00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.977
the right function or functions

90
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:11.911
out of the list.

91
00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:16.349
Indeed, you can also present a
no relevant query function,

92
00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:20.687
which the LLM can use
if it decides that none of the functions

93
00:05:20.687 --> 00:05:23.690
you have provided is relevant to the user
query.

94
00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.591
Let's take a look.

95
00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:28.027
In this example, there is a new function

96
00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:31.230
called the draw
tie function, which draws a tie.

97
00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:35.468
This is also in the utils.py file

98
00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:39.872
to keep your notebook from getting too
cluttered, here's what it looks like.

99
00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:45.678
You will specify a user query
that asks Raven to just draw a tie.

100
00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:49.615
However, you will provide
both the functions

101
00:05:49.615 --> 00:05:53.119
to Raven, both the draw clown face
and the draw tie function.

102
00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:00.960
Here's the prompt.

103
00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.297
You can see that both
the functions are present.

104
00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:08.468
Draw clown face and draw tie
and we have the user query.

105
00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:11.971
Let's send it to the LLM.

106
00:06:12.372 --> 00:06:14.006
Great. What do you notice?

107
00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:18.544
It's Raven as only use the draw
tie function and it is ignored.

108
00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:21.547
The draw clown face function
as you would expect.

109
00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:23.883
Let's now execute the call.

110
00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:25.118
Great! A tie!

111
00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:27.053
We're ready for a formal dinner.

112
00:06:27.053 --> 00:06:30.456
It's also important to note
that it's possible to combine

113
00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.727
multiple functions and parallel function
calling at the same time.

114
00:06:35.428 --> 00:06:39.799
In this example, you will ask Raven
to draw a clown and a tie.

115
00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:45.438
As before, you provide Raven with the draw
tie and draw a clown face

116
00:06:45.438 --> 00:06:46.005
function.

117
00:06:49.075 --> 00:06:52.078
Let's take a look at Raven's call.

118
00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:55.381
Since Raven was not given
any concrete requirements

119
00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:58.851
when it comes to the clown face,
it is made best guess assumptions

120
00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:02.321
about what arguments would work
best based on the default values

121
00:07:02.321 --> 00:07:05.057
for the functions
provided in the dot string,

122
00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.994
such as for the face color
and for the eye color.

123
00:07:07.994 --> 00:07:10.997
Let's call it.

124
00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:14.333
Great! A clown with the tie.

125
00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:18.171
It is at this point that it's worth
talking about the significance of the dot

126
00:07:18.171 --> 00:07:21.841
string of the functions
that you have provided, especially

127
00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:26.579
as the complexity of the function grows
and you provide multiple tools to Raven,

128
00:07:26.879 --> 00:07:30.049
the significance of the dot string becomes
more important.

129
00:07:30.483 --> 00:07:32.251
Let's show a failure case.

130
00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:35.855
Let's ask Raven
to draw a sad clown with a green head.

131
00:07:37.723 --> 00:07:37.990
When you

132
00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:41.027
take a look at the call
that doesn't look quite right.

133
00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:44.130
It gets the face color right,
but the clown is happy.

134
00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:47.700
It's possible that the reason for this
is because the dots

135
00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:50.703
strings for the functions
are not sufficiently detailed.

136
00:07:50.937 --> 00:07:53.539
Therefore,
we would need to iterate a bit to make it

137
00:07:53.539 --> 00:07:56.542
clear
what the arguments of the function does.

138
00:07:57.376 --> 00:08:01.681
You can take the prompt from earlier
and replace a description for the argument

139
00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:05.885
that has the most impact
on the perceived emotions of the clown.

140
00:08:08.221 --> 00:08:11.023
You previously
had it state that the starting

141
00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:15.027
and ending angles of the mouth
art is what this controls.

142
00:08:15.061 --> 00:08:18.130
However,
because this is very vague and doesn't

143
00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:21.133
clearly link
back to the essence of the user query,

144
00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.670
Raven was unable to understand
what this actually means.

145
00:08:24.704 --> 00:08:25.838
What you will do

146
00:08:25.838 --> 00:08:30.510
is simply replace this with a more clear
description of what this argument does.

147
00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:45.157
Such as, by adding this line.

148
00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:50.563
You will now query Raven
with this new prompt.

149
00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:54.066
What you now notice
is that there is a new argument present.

150
00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:55.535
This looks great.

151
00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:58.170
Now we have a sad clown as requested.

152
00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.607
In this example,
we observed the impact of good dot

153
00:09:01.641 --> 00:09:06.641
strings, as it can help Raven understand
how best to address the user query.

154
00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:08.848
When you notice that prompts are failing,

155
00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:12.952
you can sometimes address this
by editing the dot string to add pointers

156
00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:15.988
to make these values
more understandable to the model.

157
00:09:16.522 --> 00:09:20.293
On the other hand,
you can also add in a few short

158
00:09:20.293 --> 00:09:23.296
examples to convey this as well.

159
00:09:23.496 --> 00:09:28.301
We have now touched on multiple functions,
so let's talk about nested functions.

160
00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:33.005
An outcome of multiple functions
is that you can now define

161
00:09:33.439 --> 00:09:37.643
independent functions
where the input to one of the functions,

162
00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:42.710
such as say f2, depends
on the output of a function such as f1.

163
00:09:43.616 --> 00:09:47.553
The LLM can utilize function f1 first,

164
00:09:48.187 --> 00:09:52.124
call it with the arguments
that it extracts from the user prompt,

165
00:09:52.792 --> 00:09:56.095
keep the output
and feed that back into f2.

166
00:09:56.195 --> 00:10:00.866
This capability can sometimes
avoid multiple calls to an LLM.

167
00:10:01.367 --> 00:10:06.138
Some agentic solutions
create intermediate results with one call

168
00:10:06.539 --> 00:10:10.876
and then final results
with the second call. With nested calls,

169
00:10:11.177 --> 00:10:13.813
this can be done in one step.

170
00:10:13.813 --> 00:10:16.816
Let's take a look at making
this more concrete.

171
00:10:16.849 --> 00:10:19.318
In this example, the cloud function

172
00:10:19.318 --> 00:10:22.321
has been split into many, many parts.

173
00:10:22.321 --> 00:10:26.459
You now have multiple functions
that draws various parts of the cloud,

174
00:10:26.759 --> 00:10:29.996
such as the head, eyes,
nose, and the mouth.

175
00:10:30.229 --> 00:10:33.566
And you have a function to combine
all of them together.

176
00:10:33.866 --> 00:10:37.169
You will find these functions
in the utils.py file.

177
00:10:38.437 --> 00:10:39.538
You have simply asked

178
00:10:39.538 --> 00:10:42.775
Raven to generate the clown
with a red face,

179
00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:47.113
blue eyes,
green nose, and a black open mouth.

180
00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:50.616
This is a prompt that was addressable
with the old function.

181
00:10:51.017 --> 00:10:55.621
But let's take a look at how Raven handles
the multiple functions.

182
00:10:56.122 --> 00:11:00.993
We will simply provide the draw
head, draw eyes, draw nose, draw mouth,

183
00:11:00.993 --> 00:11:04.864
and the combination function to Raven
along with the user

184
00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:07.867
query.

185
00:11:11.270 --> 00:11:14.373
You'll notice that the response is
first called

186
00:11:14.373 --> 00:11:17.843
the draw clown face parts
such as for draw head,

187
00:11:18.411 --> 00:11:21.947
draw eyes, draw nose, draw mouth,

188
00:11:22.348 --> 00:11:26.185
and so on with the necessary arguments.

189
00:11:26.252 --> 00:11:30.022
It is then combined the output
of all of these calls

190
00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:34.293
by passing that into the combination
function that we've defined.

191
00:11:35.795 --> 00:11:38.264
Let's run it.

192
00:11:38.264 --> 00:11:38.798
Great.

193
00:11:38.798 --> 00:11:40.533
There's our clown.

194
00:11:40.533 --> 00:11:42.935
Try some variations on the prompt
yourself.

195
00:11:42.935 --> 00:11:46.172
Maybe try to add parallel nested clouds.

196
00:11:47.006 --> 00:11:50.009
This concludes
our lesson on function called variations.

197
00:11:50.342 --> 00:11:53.646
In the next lesson
you will use external functions,

198
00:11:53.979 --> 00:11:58.979
such as functions that rely on open
API specifications and API endpoints

199
00:12:00.119 --> 00:12:04.857
that will enable you to add web service
to your function calling LLM.

